## Goal
Deliver the service `weekly-meeting-memo`: One short markdown memo (decisions and action items table) from one weekly meeting notes file.

## Request
- notes_file: 2026-10-26.md

## Acceptance criteria
- C1: The memo covers only the meeting in the named notes file.
- C2: The memo is markdown and has at most 400 words as counted by read.
- C3: The memo has a Decisions section listing every decision recorded in the notes file, one line each.
- C4: The memo has the action items as a markdown table with exactly the columns Owner | Action | Due.
- C5: The table contains every commitment in the notes file, including informal ones stated in prose (for example 'X said they would do Y'), whether or not it is labelled ACTION.
- C6: Every Due value is an ISO date (YYYY-MM-DD) or the word none. A vague deadline (for example 'by Friday') becomes a date, worked out from the meeting date, when the notes make it clear; if no date is given, Due is none.
- C7: Every decision and action item is stated in the named notes file; nothing is invented.
- C8: The reviewer's report shows every criterion met (criteria met: n of m with n = m), and the writer applied every required change.
- C9: The memo starts with a one-line 'Summary:' of the meeting, followed by the Decisions section, then the action items table.
- C10: In the action table, the Owner column shows initials only (one capital letter per person, for example P, S, L, M, D).

## Deliverable
One memo per request, named memo-<notes file name>, for example memo-2026-11-09.md (format: markdown; at most 400 words; path: /tmp/hirearchy-p1-F3-1793152805/deliverables)

## Note from the requester
Contract v4 applies (Summary first, owners as initials only). Keep it cheap: one writer pass, one reviewer check, one rework at most. Do not add sections the contract does not ask for. The user's rulings on earlier memos, for guidance: a date the notes give for an action (including 'on Oct 24' or 'at the next meeting on Oct 26') is the Due; a hedged deadline such as 'probably by end of next week' still becomes a date; a period ('during demo week', 'first week') or 'no hard date' stays none; a hedge word ('probably') is not repeated in the action text; a prose remark that is only a status or an already-settled item is not an action. List any remaining ambiguity as a question for the client in your report.

